00:28:56.861 --> 00:29:00.835
Okay. This is an obvious
terrible prediction.

00:29:00.836 --> 00:29:03.280
When you look at participants,

00:29:03.281 --> 00:29:04.630
this is what it looks like.

00:29:04.631 --> 00:29:07.630
People start writing
changed unlike one third

00:29:07.631 --> 00:29:09.340
and when they have to learn

00:29:09.341 --> 00:29:11.830
two things they're
fairly close to optimal.

00:29:11.831 --> 00:29:14.125
They take three tries
and then they get

00:29:14.126 --> 00:29:19.735
richer asymptotic behavior and
then the bigger the set size,

00:29:19.736 --> 00:29:24.250
the more incremental their overall
learning curve looks like.

00:29:24.251 --> 00:29:31.870
Okay. So, this is an effect
that can be accounted by

00:29:31.871 --> 00:29:33.910
the classic form of
reinforcement learning that

00:29:33.911 --> 00:29:35.800
people usually use and then you can

00:29:35.801 --> 00:29:40.225
do all kinds of tricks on this
reinforcement learning models.

00:29:40.226 --> 00:29:42.160
For example, you can add forgetting

00:29:42.161 --> 00:29:45.200
or you can add interference
or you can add

00:29:46.230 --> 00:29:50.650
many different mechanisms and try
to see if you can still force

00:29:50.651 --> 00:29:52.630
a single reinforcement
learning model to capture

00:29:52.631 --> 00:29:55.870
the behavior and I have
not been successful.

00:29:55.871 --> 00:29:58.150
So, this is about the best
you can do if you just

00:29:58.151 --> 00:30:01.150
add some forgetting to
the Q values at each trial.

00:30:01.151 --> 00:30:04.600
You're going to get
a slight effect of set size,

00:30:04.601 --> 00:30:07.000
due to the fact that when
you're learning two things,

00:30:07.001 --> 00:30:08.995
you see them every two
trials in average.

00:30:08.996 --> 00:30:10.570
Whereas when you're
learning six things,

00:30:10.571 --> 00:30:12.160
you see them every six
trials in average.

00:30:12.161 --> 00:30:13.720
So, you have more time to forget.

00:30:13.721 --> 00:30:16.749
Okay. But it's clearly not capturing

00:30:16.750 --> 00:30:20.455
the qualitative pattern
of behavior. Yeah.

00:30:20.456 --> 00:30:26.740
&gt;&gt; So, on the white graph that
shows with you two stimuli,

00:30:26.741 --> 00:30:30.850
the blue curve, the fast
learning shown there.

00:30:30.851 --> 00:30:36.265
Is that because of generalization
between images of two things?

00:30:36.266 --> 00:30:39.985
&gt;&gt; No. So, this is the pattern
you expect with perfect memory.

00:30:39.986 --> 00:30:43.405
So, this is the first time
you see the pumpkin,

00:30:43.406 --> 00:30:45.040
second time you see the pumpkin,

00:30:45.041 --> 00:30:46.585
third time you see a pumpkin.

00:30:46.586 --> 00:30:48.280
Because you have three keys,

00:30:48.281 --> 00:30:50.860
this is the optimal button to have.

00:30:50.861 --> 00:30:53.710
So, if you have perfect memory
you'll remember I try this,

00:30:53.711 --> 00:30:55.015
I try that, I try that,

00:30:55.016 --> 00:30:57.205
and this is the correct way.

00:30:57.206 --> 00:31:00.280
&gt;&gt; So, why does perfect memory,

00:31:00.281 --> 00:31:04.805
two better with
two stimuli than with six?

00:31:04.806 --> 00:31:06.285
&gt;&gt; Well. These are humans.

00:31:06.286 --> 00:31:09.135
So, what I think this
is showing is that,

00:31:09.136 --> 00:31:11.970
when we're trying to remember
two things this is still within

00:31:11.971 --> 00:31:14.210
our working memory capacity which is

00:31:14.211 --> 00:31:17.500
able learn to use it perfectly.

00:31:17.501 --> 00:31:19.195
So, perfectly remember okay,

00:31:19.196 --> 00:31:22.390
this one for the pumpkin and
this one for a green beans.

00:31:22.391 --> 00:31:24.685
But as soon as we go
a bit beyond that,

00:31:24.686 --> 00:31:26.800
it goes beyond
our working memory capacity.

00:31:26.801 --> 00:31:28.300
So, we can't have
that perfect memory.

00:31:28.301 --> 00:31:29.650
&gt;&gt; So, that's the stimulus,

00:31:29.651 --> 00:31:32.260
just the problems with
the working memory.

00:31:32.261 --> 00:31:35.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, the reinforcement
learning is it just that

00:31:35.471 --> 00:31:38.920
because alpha is not big
enough it takes longer?

00:31:38.921 --> 00:31:42.085
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, yeah. You could
have an alpha that's bigger and

00:31:42.086 --> 00:31:46.375
a Softmax temperature that's bigger
and you could have perfect RL.

00:31:46.376 --> 00:31:48.340
The parameters are used here are

00:31:48.341 --> 00:31:51.535
the best fit parameters
to this behavior.

00:31:51.536 --> 00:31:54.555
I don't remember exactly what
it is talking about.

00:31:54.556 --> 00:31:57.480
Does that make sense? Yeah.

00:31:57.481 --> 00:31:59.740
&gt;&gt; Does that mean that
reinforcement learning is actually

00:31:59.741 --> 00:32:02.755
better than the human learning?

00:32:02.756 --> 00:32:05.080
&gt;&gt; That's going to
be very dependent on

00:32:05.081 --> 00:32:09.590
the environment and I'm going
to speak about that later.

00:32:10.620 --> 00:32:16.975
But but probably not
because if it was,

00:32:16.976 --> 00:32:20.815
then you would expect that when
you're within your capacity,

00:32:20.816 --> 00:32:24.190
you will be doing worse
than when you're outside,

00:32:24.191 --> 00:32:26.630
when you're using
reinforcement learning.

00:32:26.760 --> 00:32:30.504
&gt;&gt; If you take for the
most of the cases,

00:32:30.505 --> 00:32:34.960
at least above four, four stimulus.

00:32:34.961 --> 00:32:35.235
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:32:35.236 --> 00:32:39.550
&gt;&gt; Stimuli. RL, does
better than humans.

00:32:39.551 --> 00:32:42.580
&gt;&gt; Oh sorry. I see what want,

00:32:42.581 --> 00:32:43.855
you're plotting this
compared to this.

00:32:43.856 --> 00:32:44.725
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:32:44.726 --> 00:32:48.250
&gt;&gt; This is a game fit with
parameters fit to this.

00:32:48.251 --> 00:32:52.720
Right. So, again I could make
this model better or worse.

00:32:52.721 --> 00:32:56.020
I could put the decay to zero
and the learning rate to

00:32:56.021 --> 00:32:59.545
one and then it would be
learning perfectly, for example.

00:32:59.546 --> 00:33:02.065
So, there's no real point
in comparing.

00:33:02.066 --> 00:33:04.300
The point of comparing
those two is to say,

00:33:04.301 --> 00:33:06.565
the qualitative pattern, the spread,

00:33:06.566 --> 00:33:13.150
the way behavior changes with
set size is much more marked in

00:33:13.151 --> 00:33:14.680
humans than it is in

00:33:14.681 --> 00:33:17.335
the best fitting
reinforcement learning model

00:33:17.336 --> 00:33:19.870
that you can have.
So, that makes sense?

00:33:19.871 --> 00:33:20.310
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:33:20.311 --> 00:33:23.515
&gt;&gt; Thank you. Okay. So, the idea

00:33:23.516 --> 00:33:26.995
here is that well I think
I'm showing you is that,

00:33:26.996 --> 00:33:30.340
I can't explain the behavior
of learning in this task

00:33:30.341 --> 00:33:34.480
with only a module that has
reinforcement learning.

00:33:34.481 --> 00:33:37.810
I have to count also
for working memory.

00:33:37.811 --> 00:33:40.210
Okay. So, to do that,

00:33:40.211 --> 00:33:43.210
I developed a model
that has a module and

00:33:43.211 --> 00:33:46.690
expect that's reinforcement
learning the same as

00:33:46.691 --> 00:33:49.000
before and then that also has

00:33:49.001 --> 00:33:53.260
a working memory module
that I'll talk about later.

00:33:53.261 --> 00:33:57.310
Then I assume that learning or the
policy that participants end up

00:33:57.311 --> 00:34:02.485
taking is a mixture of
the policies of the two experts.

00:34:02.486 --> 00:34:05.080
So, it's this idea that we have
those two learning system,

00:34:05.081 --> 00:34:10.300
the decision systems and
that they're going to mix,

00:34:10.301 --> 00:34:13.190
come together for choice.

00:34:13.530 --> 00:34:16.840
Okay. So, the model
of the working memory

00:34:16.841 --> 00:34:19.885
comes from what we know
about working memory.

00:34:19.886 --> 00:34:21.640
Outside of the domain of learning and

00:34:21.641 --> 00:34:24.595
the domain of decision-making. Yeah.

00:34:24.596 --> 00:34:28.480
&gt;&gt; So, is that second, the RL works.

00:34:28.481 --> 00:34:29.245
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:34:29.246 --> 00:34:32.005
&gt;&gt; The same what you're
describing moments ago?

00:34:32.006 --> 00:34:32.490
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:34:32.491 --> 00:34:38.890
&gt;&gt; It gives better behavior than
humans a bit in some sense,

00:34:38.891 --> 00:34:44.335
it doesn't show the effectiveness
of set size as dramatically.

00:34:44.336 --> 00:34:48.520
&gt;&gt; Exactly. It's even simpler.

00:34:48.521 --> 00:34:50.215
It's this one.

00:34:50.216 --> 00:34:51.040
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:34:51.041 --> 00:34:54.835
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Okay.

00:34:54.836 --> 00:34:56.410
&gt;&gt;The assumption is that

00:34:56.411 --> 00:34:59.200
the pure reinforcement
learning literature

00:34:59.201 --> 00:35:01.660
hasn't shown any effect of decay.

00:35:01.661 --> 00:35:04.510
So, I think the forgetting is

00:35:04.511 --> 00:35:08.570
a property of working memory rather
than reinforcement learning.

00:35:08.690 --> 00:35:13.005
Okay. So, what the working
memory is doing is,

00:35:13.006 --> 00:35:16.600
I'm going to assume that we
have a fixed number of items,

00:35:16.601 --> 00:35:18.805
we can maintain off
in working memory.

00:35:18.806 --> 00:35:20.800
So, for example, three
and I'm going to

00:35:20.801 --> 00:35:23.875
parameterize that by
a capacity parameter.

00:35:23.876 --> 00:35:26.560
Well, this is the way
I am going to model.

00:35:26.561 --> 00:35:27.940
This is to say well,

00:35:27.941 --> 00:35:30.520
three out of the number

00:35:30.521 --> 00:35:32.410
of items I'm trying to
remember are going to be

00:35:32.411 --> 00:35:34.180
stored perfectly in working memory

00:35:34.181 --> 00:35:37.505
and three are not going to be stored.

00:35:37.506 --> 00:35:40.785
I can't do exactly that
for technical reasons.

00:35:40.786 --> 00:35:43.035
So, instead what I do

00:35:43.036 --> 00:35:48.900
is imagine that working memory
has a perfect policy,

00:35:48.901 --> 00:35:53.310
one trial back but
decay over time and

00:35:53.311 --> 00:35:57.030
then that the probability that I
will be able to use this item,

00:35:57.031 --> 00:36:00.030
for my decision is going to decay

00:36:00.031 --> 00:36:04.005
with set size when it
goes below capacity.

00:36:04.006 --> 00:36:10.200
Okay. So, it's at maximum if
the number of items I'm trying

00:36:10.201 --> 00:36:13.170
to remember is below
capacity and then it

00:36:13.171 --> 00:36:16.615
decreases in an inverse way over.

00:36:16.616 --> 00:36:19.135
&gt;&gt; So, just the working memory

00:36:19.136 --> 00:36:22.450
represents a policy and does
that mean that when we see

00:36:22.451 --> 00:36:26.620
an image there in the rectangle
on the left and one of

00:36:26.621 --> 00:36:29.230
the three choices
decide that is that's

00:36:29.231 --> 00:36:32.710
the correct choice or it's
the choice that the user shows?

00:36:32.711 --> 00:36:40.390
&gt;&gt; So, that would be it's
the last trials information.

00:36:40.391 --> 00:36:41.560
&gt;&gt; Which maybe wrong.

00:36:41.561 --> 00:36:43.840
&gt;&gt; Which may be wrong. Well, it won't

00:36:43.841 --> 00:36:46.375
be wrong because the feedback
is deterministic,

00:36:46.376 --> 00:36:53.110
but it will remember to not
pick something that was wrong.

00:36:53.111 --> 00:36:55.705
&gt;&gt; So it's not the optimal policy.

00:36:55.706 --> 00:37:00.220
It's just some information of
what they did and also I guess is

00:37:00.221 --> 00:37:02.500
implied that it remembers

00:37:02.501 --> 00:37:04.720
whether they were
rewarded or not for that.

00:37:04.721 --> 00:37:12.130
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, it's joining
a bit, all of the S actually.

00:37:12.131 --> 00:37:16.045
So, I can go into a bit more detail
behind more of it.

00:37:16.046 --> 00:37:20.530
Essentially, our modeling,
the working memory the same

00:37:20.531 --> 00:37:22.000
way as I do

00:37:22.001 --> 00:37:25.375
the reinforcement learning model
with a learning rate of one.

00:37:25.376 --> 00:37:32.965
So, it has this compressed information
but it also has this decay,

00:37:32.966 --> 00:37:35.125
which means that the
policy gets worse

00:37:35.126 --> 00:37:39.650
the more intervening trials coming.

00:37:40.470 --> 00:37:44.140
This tells me how likely I am to use

00:37:44.141 --> 00:37:46.120
working memory versus
reinforcement learning

00:37:46.121 --> 00:37:48.610
and it decreases with set size,

00:37:48.611 --> 00:37:52.675
and this is going to go into
the mixture weight here,

00:37:52.676 --> 00:37:58.600
telling me how likely I am to use
working memory versus [inaudible].

00:37:58.601 --> 00:37:59.950
Okay. So, this model has

00:37:59.951 --> 00:38:02.230
a few free parameters that I

00:38:02.231 --> 00:38:04.885
can fit again
two participants behavior.

00:38:04.886 --> 00:38:07.090
Then when I have fit
those parameters,

00:38:07.091 --> 00:38:10.495
I can simulate them all again
and compare to participants,

00:38:10.496 --> 00:38:13.150
and you see that
essentially we capture

00:38:13.151 --> 00:38:16.000
the behavior much
better than we did with

00:38:16.001 --> 00:38:19.150
the previous reinforcement
learning models.

00:38:19.151 --> 00:38:21.370
Okay? So, this is

00:38:21.371 --> 00:38:25.795
just one step of showing that
this model is helpful for

00:38:25.796 --> 00:38:32.400
understanding human learning and
after many others to validate

00:38:32.401 --> 00:38:34.470
the fact that we really
need those two modules

00:38:34.471 --> 00:38:38.640
to explain human learning,

00:38:38.641 --> 00:38:40.350
but I won't bore you
with details. Yeah.

00:38:40.351 --> 00:38:45.520
&gt;&gt; The natural thing for me to
do is the next step would be to

00:38:45.521 --> 00:38:48.250
vary another parameter and vary
their own word that somebody

00:38:48.251 --> 00:38:51.160
gets from being successful as that,

00:38:51.161 --> 00:38:53.140
but I know that in psychology

00:38:53.141 --> 00:38:55.255
where experiments
aren't often rewarded.

00:38:55.256 --> 00:38:57.085
Have you run that experiment?

00:38:57.086 --> 00:38:59.815
&gt;&gt; So, I've run a version
of this experiment,

00:38:59.816 --> 00:39:02.260
I actually think I'll
be talking about this.

00:39:02.261 --> 00:39:05.080
I've run a version of
this experiment where we do

00:39:05.081 --> 00:39:10.120
manipulate the reward we
get for being correct.

00:39:10.121 --> 00:39:12.055
So, we still have zero versus

00:39:12.056 --> 00:39:17.215
deterministic information as to
whether the output is correct,

00:39:17.216 --> 00:39:20.695
and then we vary one
points versus two points

00:39:20.696 --> 00:39:26.640
probabilistically and
it's a mixed success

00:39:26.641 --> 00:39:29.370
because it's very hard
to make people actually

00:39:29.371 --> 00:39:33.810
care about reward
especially in such a long,

00:39:33.811 --> 00:39:37.245
participants here do 900 trials.

00:39:37.246 --> 00:39:40.420
So, any single point they get

00:39:40.421 --> 00:39:43.345
a single trial does not
have very much influenced.

00:39:43.346 --> 00:39:44.950
So, we end up seeing that

00:39:44.951 --> 00:39:48.145
people are not paying
that much attention to it.

00:39:48.146 --> 00:39:50.710
So, we should probably work harder to

00:39:50.711 --> 00:39:53.815
try to incentivize those points more,

00:39:53.816 --> 00:39:55.390
if we want to go there,

00:39:55.391 --> 00:39:57.595
but I haven't done too much more.

00:39:57.596 --> 00:40:01.765
&gt;&gt; Since we play with the order
of images now to show that

00:40:01.766 --> 00:40:05.680
this really does happen not
only on average like this,

00:40:05.681 --> 00:40:09.880
but if you keep on seeing images
that they fall perfectly,

00:40:09.881 --> 00:40:12.145
and then there is a drop
in performance and so,

00:40:12.146 --> 00:40:14.740
because you can actually predict

00:40:14.741 --> 00:40:17.740
more complicated things for
time based on this one.

00:40:17.741 --> 00:40:20.050
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, I think my model for

00:40:20.051 --> 00:40:22.780
Q memory so far is
pretty bad actually.

00:40:22.781 --> 00:40:24.700
I think it's an average model,

00:40:24.701 --> 00:40:27.145
and it's something I'm working on.

00:40:27.146 --> 00:40:32.230
It's been very difficult to
find a better prediction of,

00:40:32.231 --> 00:40:36.460
so what's difficult for me
right now is to say I have

00:40:36.461 --> 00:40:41.125
a capacity of three and I'm
trying to remember six things,

00:40:41.126 --> 00:40:44.530
which three am I going to prioritize?

00:40:44.531 --> 00:40:47.920
There's no very good information
on there telling

00:40:47.921 --> 00:40:51.730
me how this is happening,

00:40:51.731 --> 00:40:54.265
and this is something I
can try to look at here,

00:40:54.266 --> 00:40:57.280
but it's been actually
extremely difficult to find

00:40:57.281 --> 00:41:01.120
any pattern in the way
participants are doing that.

00:41:01.121 --> 00:41:03.520
&gt;&gt; But it's how they
behave if they keep on

00:41:03.521 --> 00:41:05.350
getting the same three
images for a while,

00:41:05.351 --> 00:41:06.850
and then the fourth, and

00:41:06.851 --> 00:41:09.040
then the same three for
a while, and then the fifth,

00:41:09.041 --> 00:41:10.720
different ways of showing them

00:41:10.721 --> 00:41:13.120
information and see how
long it takes them to

00:41:13.121 --> 00:41:16.975
learn because then you got
very different model behaviors,

00:41:16.976 --> 00:41:19.420
and then even using the
model that you already have.

00:41:19.421 --> 00:41:19.795
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:41:19.796 --> 00:41:21.370
&gt;&gt; You can see what it fits better.

00:41:21.371 --> 00:41:23.930
&gt;&gt; Yeah. The primary is that,

00:41:24.150 --> 00:41:29.690
so these are all experiments
with thought of doing.

00:41:29.691 --> 00:41:31.555
There's many difficulties.

00:41:31.556 --> 00:41:33.940
Some of them are that we presume that

00:41:33.941 --> 00:41:36.910
actually the weight we
give to working memory.

00:41:36.911 --> 00:41:39.025
So I go over this,

00:41:39.026 --> 00:41:43.420
but you see that the maximum
here is not one,

00:41:43.421 --> 00:41:46.790
with actually a parameter two.

00:41:48.570 --> 00:41:52.420
We think that people don't have

00:41:52.421 --> 00:41:56.950
a fixed mixture weight even
for a given policy here.

00:41:56.951 --> 00:41:59.380
But over time they might say like

00:41:59.381 --> 00:42:03.040
as reinforcement learning
becomes better at predicting,

00:42:03.041 --> 00:42:05.170
reinforcement learning is
going to learn slower,

00:42:05.171 --> 00:42:08.140
but after sometime it's going to have

00:42:08.141 --> 00:42:11.635
learnt well and not be
sensitive to decay,

00:42:11.636 --> 00:42:14.485
which means it's going to become
a better predictor over time,

00:42:14.486 --> 00:42:16.960
at which point because working
memory is [inaudible] ,

00:42:16.961 --> 00:42:20.455
we might start choosing
it last even if we can.

00:42:20.456 --> 00:42:22.540
So, there are
very complicated dynamics

00:42:22.541 --> 00:42:24.235
in time actually happening there,

00:42:24.236 --> 00:42:28.720
which is why it's more
complicated than it

00:42:28.721 --> 00:42:30.310
looks like to actually understand

00:42:30.311 --> 00:42:33.535
well what working
memory is like. Yeah.

00:42:33.536 --> 00:42:38.290
&gt;&gt; Have you looked at just with
existing data how the probability

00:42:38.291 --> 00:42:40.390
of being correct varies with

00:42:40.391 --> 00:42:43.075
the amount of time since they've
seen that particular image?

00:42:43.076 --> 00:42:45.340
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, yeah.
It's in the paper.

00:42:45.341 --> 00:42:46.840
I didn't put it in this presentation,

00:42:46.841 --> 00:42:48.880
but there's a big effect
of delay that

00:42:48.881 --> 00:42:52.210
interacts both with
time and with set size.

00:42:52.211 --> 00:42:56.710
So, the effect of delay is
bigger in higher set sizes.

00:42:56.711 --> 00:43:02.890
So, there's more forgetting and it

00:43:02.891 --> 00:43:06.190
