01:17:01.511 --> 01:17:05.840
that is super simple experiment,

01:17:06.690 --> 01:17:10.030
where people just see
a stream of ones and zeros,

01:17:10.031 --> 01:17:12.520
I think it's black and white
dots or something like that,

01:17:12.521 --> 01:17:15.520
but for all intents and
purposes it's a stream

01:17:15.521 --> 01:17:21.350
of binary variable randomly
picked with probably 2.5.

01:17:23.010 --> 01:17:26.305
If you just look at
their reaction times,

01:17:26.306 --> 01:17:31.120
you see that, if you have one, zero,

01:17:31.121 --> 01:17:34.660
one, zero, what people have to

01:17:34.661 --> 01:17:38.380
say is press right for
one and zero for less.

01:17:38.381 --> 01:17:40.810
So, it's no learning,
no decision line,

01:17:40.811 --> 01:17:42.625
like just press as fast as possible,

01:17:42.626 --> 01:17:44.680
and following what you
see on the screen.

01:17:44.681 --> 01:17:49.780
What you see is that the various
local structure in the sequence,

01:17:49.781 --> 01:17:51.490
even though it's random,
it's probably one, zero,

01:17:51.491 --> 01:17:54.175
and zero, will be much
faster and spend answering

01:17:54.176 --> 01:17:55.660
one than they will be at inserting

01:17:55.661 --> 01:17:57.790
zeros after that or if there's 1.1,

01:17:57.791 --> 01:18:00.025
they've been much faster
than answering one.

01:18:00.026 --> 01:18:02.920
So, in that sense that's
exactly overfitting saying like

01:18:02.921 --> 01:18:07.060
this local structure it's more
regular than we think it is,

01:18:07.061 --> 01:18:08.740
and we're predicting the next one

01:18:08.741 --> 01:18:10.270
based on this very local structure.

01:18:10.271 --> 01:18:13.940
That's overfitting and we
would integrate this way too.

01:18:15.150 --> 01:18:24.730
The type of thing I do goes
a bit beyond that because we're

01:18:24.731 --> 01:18:30.400
