RL computation cannot happen over

01:25:33.716 --> 01:25:38.230
those two state and action spaces
in parallel and that will

01:25:38.231 --> 01:25:43.390
explain a whole bunch of behavior
in terms of how people learn.

01:25:43.391 --> 01:25:44.700
&gt;&gt; So, it seems to me like

01:25:44.701 --> 01:25:48.435
the red experimental variation
this world would be how

01:25:48.436 --> 01:25:50.760
human decision-makers operate in

01:25:50.761 --> 01:25:52.170
dynamic systems that are

01:25:52.171 --> 01:25:54.270
rapidly changing versus
those that aren't.

01:25:54.271 --> 01:25:54.480
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:25:54.481 --> 01:25:57.515
&gt;&gt; Because basically these rules

01:25:57.516 --> 01:25:59.980
become more costly
in those situations.

01:25:59.981 --> 01:26:02.480
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

01:26:03.240 --> 01:26:07.885
So, that's going to hit against,

01:26:07.886 --> 01:26:10.450
so when you do that you
have to have a way to

01:26:10.451 --> 01:26:13.150
identify whether people
are selecting those assets

01:26:13.151 --> 01:26:15.460
or those rules to convince
yourself that they are doing

01:26:15.461 --> 01:26:18.970
that and that's going
to be even harder.

01:26:18.971 --> 01:26:21.460
It's already very hard in
non-moving environments.

01:26:21.461 --> 01:26:23.770
It's going to be even harder
in moving environments.

01:26:23.771 --> 01:26:26.200
So, that's the point of

01:26:26.201 --> 01:26:27.730
studying complex human learning

01:26:27.731 --> 01:26:29.230
is that you always
have this trade-off

01:26:29.231 --> 01:26:34.390
where you lead more
complex experiments to see

01:26:34.391 --> 01:26:37.570
more interesting behavior but
that comes from the drawback

01:26:37.571 --> 01:26:41.710
of being very hard to infer what
the participants are doing.

01:26:41.711 --> 01:26:43.690
So, but yeah, you're right, yeah.

01:26:43.691 --> 01:26:45.610
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

01:26:45.611 --> 01:26:46.870
&gt;&gt; Let's thank Ann

01:26:46.871 --> 01:26:58.320
[inaudible].

